WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:07.890 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  how much are we willing to forgive when  we really like a person Peter Parker's a  normal guy so grounded despite his  superpowers he has an innate likability  that draws me towards him  I love his sarcastic insults as he  punches enemies and wry observations as  he swings through the city I never get  to sightsee he's fun but he also lacks  restraint beating the stuffing out of  ordinary criminals seems a bit excessive  no and then the amazing spider-man 2 he  bugs cell phone towers for his own  revenge so is he a good guy or just  another powerful man who sets his own  rules and that question of forgiveness  extends to the action - I like  spider-man and is that a snappy best in  this latest adventure I wanted to spend  time with him hear him belt out insults  while smacking down his evil minded  folks but should I forgive the game's  problems just because I find the main  character so endearing it's not an easy  question at times I did overlook the  swirling camera that struggles to  properly frame the action and the sloppy  locomotion as I swung across Manhattan  after all why get hung up on the finicky  movement when it feels so freeing to  soar above the traffic below but at the  hours flew by the problems that came  more noticeable the combat is so simple  more reliant on quick time button  presses than actual skill that it soon  became tiresome to knock my opponents  heads in and similar to what we've seen  in the Batman Arkham series only even  more rudimentary than the Dark Knight's  fisticuffs and without the bone crushing  impact that makes you cringe  [Music]  Fite require a little more than slamming  on attack and dodging when enemies glow  red and I need more depth in my super  heroic conquests the side missions also  require their fair share of forgiveness  Manhattan is a crime-ridden City where  citizens are confronted early and often  by the criminal underbelly spire man  must stop these heinous acts to keep his  hero level high so you're compelled to  stop high-speed chases and robberies  before the city turns against you it's a  fine idea because spider-man should  strive for the greater good but even  this simple idea falters for one the  side missions themselves aren't  interesting rescuing people from  smoke-filled buildings highlights how  clunky the game is and enclosed spaces  and fighting regular enemies is too easy  to be interesting the punishment system  wasn't a real that turns either what did  I care people didn't like me sure a task  force would hunt me down but it was easy  enough to avoid their strikes plus the  story missions pitted me as the villain  anyway  no matter how much good I accomplished  so the side missions are a bust  still if not like the amazing spider-man  2 lacks fonts  I did enjoy swinging through the city  there are comic book pages and posters  sprinkled throughout and even though I  don't read comic books in real life it  was cool to check out one in Stanley  shop it's just a world I want to exist  in to spend time with spider-man sees  various suits and meet as many members  the amazing spider-man 2 is worse  failing is that it's expected we've seen  countless open-world games in this  manner including many already starring  spider-man sort of Warne surprises but  it's all a man of your forgiveness  spider-man is so likable that it's  possible to overlook the simplicity of  the combat and the tedium of the side  missions  there's nothing majorly wrong with the  game after all this is not a heck of a  lot right with it either  [Music]

